rity-review` and a `## Checks` body. It never builds, tests or runs the repository's code. It runs, when they are installed: gitleaks over the changed files with `--redact`; gosec on changed Go packages with `-nosec`; and semgrep with `--metrics=off` and `--disable-nosem`, using only a rule file already in the repository that the change does not touch, or the sidecar's. Suppressions the change adds are reviewed as part of the change. It skips a missing tool and says so, never downloads rules or vulnerability data, and never copies a secret it finds into its report (file, line and kind only). Each finding's first line starts with `[injection]`, `[authz]`, `[secret]`, `[dependency]` or `[other]`. Critical and high block, medium only when it is one of the four kinds with a nameable exploit, low and info never; `needs-human` only when there is nothing to review. The starter override is Opus 5.5 (#8) with the default 30m timeout and no `when:`. The role's `aid` (#205) runs gitleaks, gosec and semgrep in controller code before the agent (Reviewer loop, step 3); the prompt reads `aid.jsonl` first and runs the tools itself only when it is absent. It ships a `Dockerfile` (the default ard-agent image plus the pinned tools), so it runs under Docker.\n+**The starter security reviewer (`reviewers/security-review`, #43).** `.ard/reviewers/security-review/prompt.txt` looks for injection, authz gaps, secrets and unsafe dependencies the change introduces or makes reachable, plus other weaknesses. It has the same untrusted inputs, Trust rules (steering text in the change is a blocking `high` finding), write rules and role-file format as eng-review, with `role: security-review` and a `## Checks` body. It never builds, tests or runs the repository's code. It runs, when they are installed: gitleaks over the changed files with `--redact`; gosec on changed Go packages with `-nosec`; and semgrep with `--metrics=off` and `--disable-nosem`, using only a rule file already in the repository that the change does not touch, or the sidecar's. Suppressions the change adds are reviewed as part of the change. It skips a missing tool and says so, never downloads rules or vulnerability data, and never copies a secret it finds into its report (file, line and kind only). Each finding's first line starts with `[injection]`, `[authz]`, `[secret]`, `[dependency]` or `[other]`. Critical and high block, medium only when it is one of the four kinds with a nameable exploit, low and info never; `needs-human` only when there is nothing to review. The starter override is Opus 5.5 (#8) with the default 30m timeout and no `when:`. The role's `aid` (#205) runs gitleaks, gosec and semgrep in controller code before the agent (Reviewer loop, step 3); the prompt reads `aid.jsonl` first and runs the tools itself only when it is absent. It ships a `Dockerfile` (the default ard-agent image plus the pinned tools), so it runs under Docker. The same fetched-file check applies, and an unverified fetched file is a high finding; it still never runs repository code (#301).\n \n **The starter license reviewer (`reviewers/license-review`, #44).** `.ard/reviewers/license-review/prompt.txt` checks that every dependency a PR adds or upgrades has a license on the owners' allowlist. The allowlist is one SPDX id per line (`#` comments; `A OR B` passes when any operand is listed, `A AND B` only when all are, `A WITH E` only when listed verbatim; no per-package exceptions). It is read from the sidecar's `licenses/allowlist.txt` when the role has a sidecar and that file exists, else from `allowlist.txt` in the role directory (`$ARD_ROLE_DIR`, read from the config snapshot on `main`, under a protected path). An allowlist found in the PR, the diff, the issue or `.ard/<issue#>/` is ignored, and an unreadable or empty one ends `needs-human`. For Go it runs `go-licenses check` and `go-licenses report` when the tool is on the host `PATH` (optional; it never installs tools), else falls back to `go mod downlo